; Leg BF -- PORTING.md SS16ag's errno question, treatment (CKFERR, -dV9K_FAST_ERRNO) arm, run 2 of 2.
;
; Treatment repeated, for the same reason as BD.
;
; The A/B is CKERMITW (as shipped, 205,256 bytes, errno through the library's
; far call) against CKFERR (-dV9K_FAST_ERRNO, 204,888, errno through
; v9k_errnop).  Both binaries are byte-identical to the ones SS16ag's MAME
; legs measured -- CKERMITW to legs AP/AQ, CKFERR to legs AL/AN -- so this is
; a continuation of that experiment at a rate MAME cannot reach, not a fresh
; one.  Under MAME at 9600 the treatment was 98 ms SLOWER.
;
; WHY 38400 CAN SETTLE WHAT 9600 COULD NOT, and it is structural rather than
; a matter of precision: at 9600 the byte time is 1,040 us against ~485 us of
; foreground, so there are 555 us of SLACK per byte for a per-byte saving to
; hide in.  At 38400 the byte time is 260 us and the foreground is BEHIND
; which is exactly why rxpeak sits at 2,581 there and at 299 at 9600.
;
; TWO LEGS PER ARM, and that is not caution for its own sake: SS16ag's
; control arm put two runs of ONE binary 321 ms apart while its other two
; arms held to 1 ms and 5 ms, with no explanation found.  One pair does not
; establish this harness's resolution.
;
; THE COMPARISON IS ONLY VALID IF THE ARMS ARE PROTOCOL-IDENTICAL: rxbytes
; equal, same packet count, same retransmissions.  A leg that retransmits
; differently is not comparable and is re-run, not adjusted.  SS16ag leg AM
; is the worked example of excluding one.
;
; DECISION RULE, STATED IN ADVANCE so it is not fitted afterwards.  If the
; treatment arm is faster by more than the within-arm spread, make
; V9K_FAST_ERRNO the default and say so in PORTING.md SS16ag.  If it is not,
; the change has failed on both instruments and should come OUT of the tree
; rather than stay as a permanent maybe.

set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing cautious
set block 3
log packets s16ahBF.pkt
send rcvbf.dat
statistics
